{"_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Designing Data Warehouses", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 11, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 3, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A2.1", "type": "level"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"content": "These are words you use to talk about designing data warehouses!", "type": "text"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Describe common activities at work to a visitor using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations. Understand information about data and its structure in simple spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Identifies the expressions and vocabulary necessary for the description of places of work and jobs in common written and spoken situations.   Describes common activities related to Software Programming through the use of appropriate expressions, grammar and vocabulary in written or spoken situations according to the abilities related to this level.", "type": "secondary_1"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le03ob11re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Connectors Review", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "Use <b>connectors</b> to link words, phrases and sentences.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Remember that connectors are words you use to link words, phrases and sentences in a text. Remember that connectors can be <b><i>coordinating conjunctions,</b></i> such as <b><i>and,</i> or,</b> and <b><i>but</b></i>, that connect items that belong to a same grammatical type.<br><br>  Examples: <br> <i>We need to implement <u>a data warehouse</u> <b>and</b> <u>a data mart</u> for each dependency of the company (noun and noun)<br><br>  We don't know if the database is <u>useless</u> <b>or</b> <u>outdated.</u> (adjective and adjective)<br><br>  We don't need <u>to change</u> the database <b>but</b> <u>to update</u> it. (verb and verb)<br><br></i>  Remember that conjunctions do not always need a comma before them, but connectors such as linking adverbs need a comma, a semicolon or a period before them.<br><br>  Linking adverbs show a mental relationship between two sentences or clauses. Let's look at some of them.<br><br>  <b>Contrast:</b> by using these kind of connectors, you emphasize differences between ideas; some of these connectors are the following: <b>on the other hand, by comparison, however, in contrast.</b><br><br>  Example:<br> <i>Samantha knows how to work with data warehouses. <b>However,</b> she is out of the country right now.</i> <br><br>  <b>Similarity:</b> You use these types of connectors when you compare and add information, for example, <b>in the same way, also and similarly.</b><br><br><br>  Example:<br> <i>Data warehouses keep all the information of the company in one place. <b>Similarly,</b> data marts compile the data of each dependency of the company.</i><br><br>  <b>Result:</b> You can use these connectors to show the consequences or the conclusions of a topic. Among these kind of connectors, we find <b>as a result, therefore, as a consequence, and accordingly.</b><br><br>   <i>Data warehouses compile all the company's data; <b>therefore</b>, you need to allow all data marts to share data with the warehouse.</i><br><br>  <b>Sequencing:</b> You can use them to give an order and coherence to your ideas, for example, <b>to continue, to begin with, and finally.</b><br><br> Example:<br> <i><b>To begin with</b>, there are many ways to keep your data sound and safe without the use of data warehouses\u2026</i>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "To start with, I agree with you. However, what kind of design are we going to work with?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Thus, here I have 2 possible designs we can work with if you don't mind. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Even though we have the schema, I think the very first thing we need to define is what factors are taking part in it.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Zero Conditional with the if clause in negative", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "Use <b>zero conditional</b> to express information about specific situations that commonly have a cause and effect.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "In order to analyze the zero conditional, we must know what the conditional mood is.<br><br>  We can see the conditional mood as a set of sentences showing the relation between facts (real or hypothetical) and their conditions. <br><br>  Therefore, a conditional sentence must have two clauses or ideas, a main clause and a conditional clause (if clause). <br><br>  We can use the zero conditional  with the if clause in negative form to express ideas that even though are always true, have a negative condition. <br><br>  Remember that both ideas (clauses) in zero conditional have the verbs in present form; the conditional clause goes with \u201cif\u201d or \u201cwhen,\u201d and the main clause goes in simple present. <br><br>  Structure: <br><br> <ol> <li>. If/when + present simple negative + , + present simple, <br> <li> Present simple + if/when + present simple negative. <br><br></ol>   Examples: <br><br>  <li><b>if you don't want</b> to share the data stored in the data mart with the data warehouse, you need to carry more time consuming processes to keep track of it.<br>   <li>Store all the data of the company in the data warehouse <b>if you don't want</b> to deal with unrelated data problems in a future.<br><br></i>  Notice that when the if clause goes first in order, you need to add a comma \u201c,\u201d to separate both ideas; but when you start the idea with the main clause, you don't need to add a comma to separate the ideas.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Here I have 2 possible designs we can work with if you don't mind. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "If 