g your own Data warehouse", "type": "text"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Take Away", "type": "group_name"}], "type": "group"}, {"content": {"incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "Whoops! Something important is missing.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time...", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}, {"content": "", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct!", "allowed_attempts": 2}, "type": "feedback"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment.", "type": "text"}], "type": "post_instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Reading", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Writing", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Speaking", "type": "skill"}], "type": "learning_skills"}], "id": 11, "type": "DWS"}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}], "type": "lab_options"}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "id": 0, "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "contents": [{"value": {"coverimg": "enpe7n3le03ob11re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"time": "100", "text": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>David:</strong>\u00a0Ok, guys. We need to finish the schema of how our data warehouse is going to look and behave if we don't want to have future problems with it.<br /> <strong>Neal:</strong>\u00a0To start with, we can set it as read-only so that no one can modify the data. Anyway, what kind of design are we going to work with?\u00a0</p>", "id": 0, "image": "enpe7n3le03ob11re2img01.jpg"}, {"time": "20000", "text": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>David:</strong>\u00a0That's a very good question, Neal. And that's why I called for this meeting because if we don't define a schema today, the possibilities to move forward with the project are almost null. Thus, here I have 2 possible designs we can work with if you don't mind. The first one is the star schema, and the other option is the snowflake one.\u00a0<br /><strong>Roxana:</strong>\u00a0Sorry to interrupt, but even though we have the schema, I think the very first thing we need to define is the factors that take part in it; for example, facts, measures, dimensions, and hierarchies. If we don't take them into account, the schema is useless.\u00a0<br /> <strong>David:</strong>\u00a0Roxana, I see some new faces here, why don't you explain to them what those factors are about.\u00a0<br /> </p>", "id": 1, "image": "enpe7n3le03ob11re2img02.jpg"}, {"time": "73000", "text": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><strong>Roxana:</strong>\u00a0Sure! Guys, remember that a fact in a data warehouse scheme is the focus of it; you know, things like sales or purchases. Additionally, the measures are the same attributes for each fact, for example, the revenues or profits from the sales. Similarly, we talk about dimensions, which are the minimal parts in which we can divide a fact, such as dates, products or stores. On the other hand, we have the hierarchies, which are the same dependencies we have in the company, like the sales or the HR departments, which, by the way, don't share their data if they don't have a data mart in their dependencies.\u00a0<br /> <strong>David:</strong>\u00a0Thanks, Roxana. In order to continue with our agenda\u2026</p>", "id": 2, "image": "enpe7n3le03ob11re2img03.jpg"}], "audiosrc": "enpe7n3le03ob11re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "title": "", "id": 1, "type": "audio"}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar"}]}